The following text discusses how to configure ENHANCED SECURITY, NIS and ASE.

My environment consists of a number of clusters that together run a
distributed TMN (Telecommunications Management Network) application. 
The requirements for uptime are high. User management is done with 
XIsso and XSysAdmin. They support NIS.

1 ASE

ASE means Available Server Environment and implements failover on DU.
Two or more machines may share a SCSI bus. A service is defined as some
processes and mountpoints. A service may be moved between the members of
an ASE cluster by moving the mount point. A servise can only be present on
one member at a time. A service can be assigned an IP adress. The machine
currently running the service adds this IP adress as an alias in its 
network interface (a machine may have more than one IP adress).

2 NIS and ENHANCED SECURITY

In Enhanced Security, a record is kept of each authentication. Enhanced 
Secutity works together with NIS by having all the information normally
stored in /tcb/files moved to the nis map prpasswd for the accounts
controlled by NIS. All in all, this means that if the NIS master server
becomes unavailable, it is not possible to do log on or off. This is 
considered a single point of failure even though the users already logged
on are able to continue their work.

3 NIS and ASE

In a cluster system, everything must be able to continue as normally even
if one of the members becomes unavailable. We will have to cluster'ise
NIS.

3.1 Cluster adress

The NIS server must have a cluster adress so that all clients and all the
slave servers can carry on unaffected by a failover.

3.2 Slave servers

Although the ASE setup must support slave servers as well as dumb clients,
it may still be a matter of taste what to use. 

Enhanced Security updates NIS every time anybody logs on or off. This makes
a lot of updating if there are many slaves. 

If a host does not run a slave, it will be literally down in case of
net outage since everything will hang in NIS (for example ls -l). 

Minimally, a machine that is a cluster member must also be a slave 
because cluster members are supposed to be able to act reasonably in
case of net outage.

4 Suggestion

4.1 On the cluster

4.1.1 Service Config

An ASE disk service is configured as follows:

o  Mountpoint: /var/yp/src
o  IP name: tmn_nis

The IP name must be added to /etc/hosts of all clients.

4.1.2 Start Script

The start script of the ASE service will stop NIS, reconfigure as NIS master, 
then restart NIS.

> /sbin/init.d/nis stop
> NIS_DOMAIN=`/usr/sbin/rcmgr get NIS_DOMAIN`
> /usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_TYPE MASTER
> /usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_ARGS "-s -S $NIS_DOMAIN,localhost"
> /usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_PASSWDD YES
> /sbin/init.d/nis start

4.1.3 Stop Script

The stop script of the ASE service will stop NIS, reconfigure as NIS slave,
then restart NIS.

This is done in the BOOTING as well as RUNNING section of the stop script
so that when rebooting a member that crashed when running NIS master causes
the member to come up as NIS slave.

> NIS_DOMAIN=`/usr/sbin/rcmgr get NIS_DOMAIN`
> /sbin/init.d/nis stop
> /usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_TYPE SLAVE
> /usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_ARGS "-s -S $NIS_DOMAIN,localhost"
> /usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_PASSWDD NO
> /sbin/init.d/nis start

4.1.4 /sbin/init.d/nis

There is not any reason for running this since ASE manages the NIS processing.
Remove the links from /sbin/init.d/rc?.d

4.1.5 nissetup as MASTER (on one selected member)

Remember to copy /etc/hosts, /etc/passwd and so on to /var/yp/src. 

Run nissetup, assign the domain. Other options are not important as 
the ASE scripts will reconfigure NIS anyway.

4.1.6 nissetup as SLAVE (on the other members)

Run nissetup, assign the domain, Enter tmn_nis as server. Other options 
are not so important as the ASE scripts will reconfigure NIS anyway.

Reboot the slave or run the commands of the stop script.


4.1.7 /var/yp/Makefile

Change the makedbm command to add the cluster server as NIS server.

#MAKEDBM=$(YPDIR)/makedbm
MAKEDBM=$(YPDIR)/makedbm -m tmn_nis

Do not disable push of NIS maps. login and passwd seem to wait for the 
update to come from the server. (or rather, they loop around in 'yppoll'
(i think) waiting for the change to become visible). See 4.1.8

Run Make

4.1.9 ypservers

The names of the NIS servers are kept in the map ypservers. This has to be
updated each time a new slave server is added. 

The problem is that if a server is down, push will hang. Then nobody will
be able to log in anyway because the slave servers are updated one by one. 

The following script, run by root crontab every minute (you may do it less 
often) pings the servers, then looks if they are up and updates the 
ypservers map. The script checks if /var/yp/src/ypservers is present,
if not the service is not owned by this host.

Then /var/yp/src/ypservers is also a convenient place to put the names of
the servers.

> #!/sbin/sh
> PATH=$PATH:/bin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/usr/sbin:/var/yp
> 
> SERVERFILE=/var/yp/src/ypservers
> TIMEOUT=10
> MAPDIR=/tmp/ypservers$$
> SERVICE=tmn_nis
> 
> [ -f $SERVERFILE ] || exit 0
> mkdir $MAPDIR || exit 0
> 
> for Y in `cat $SERVERFILE`
> do
>         if ping -c 1 $Y >/dev/null 2>&1
>         then
>                 > $MAPDIR/$Y
>         fi >> $MAPFILE &
> done
> 
> sleep $TIMEOUT
> 
> ls $MAPDIR | awk '{print $0, $0}' | makedbm \
> -m $SERVICE - /var/yp/`domainname`/ypservers
> yppush ypservers
> rm -rf $MAPDIR


4.2 On other hosts

4.2.1 /etc/hosts

Add an entry for tmn_nis

4.2.1 nissetup

Set up the hosts with nissetup (according to your slave-or-not policy), 
the master server is tmn_nis

5 Issues

5.1 XIsso and XSysAdmin

These programs cause problems. They perform a test whether the host is
the NIS master server. They seem to compare tmn_nis with `hostname`

A fix for this is to let tmn_nis be an alias to `hostname` in /etc/hosts.
However, the ASE start and stop script must be able to do this since
tmn_nis on the standby will be an adress of its own. 

The following commands

ON=NIS_MASTER
OFF=NIS_SLAVE
HOSTS=/etc/hosts
cp $HOSTS $HOSTS.old
sed  -e 's/^#\(.*\)\(# '$ON'\)/\1\2/' -e 's/\(^[^#].*# '$OFF'\)/#\1/'\
< $HOSTS.old > $HOSTS


Will edit /etc/hosts so that lines marked with the comment # NIS_MASTER
will be active and lines with the comment # NIS_SLAVE will be commented
out.

129.25.30.137   rup.dscc.dk            rup tmn_nis     # NIS_MASTER
#129.25.30.137   rup.dscc.dk           rup             # NIS_SLAVE
#129.25.30.134   tmn_nis.dscc.dk    tmn_nis   # NIS_SLAVE


These may be put into the ASE scripts.


5.2 ypxfr in crontabs

Nissetup configures the crontabs on the slave servers to run ypxfr. This
is not done on the master server. It does not seem to harm having this 
active on the master as well as on the slaves.

Just remember to configure the crontab on the master as well.

When running without push, configure ypxfr_1perhour to get prpasswd and
prpasswd_nonsecure as well.

6 Test

The test includes one cluster and one node that runs a SLAVE server.

6.1 Perform login, then change password on each
6.2 Run xon tmn_nis /usr/tcb/bin/XIsso from the non-cluster node.
6.3 Shut down the current NIS server
6.4 Run 6.1, 6.2 once again

7 Further suggestions

User management is done like this:

xon tmn_nis /usr/tcb/bin/XIsso
xon tmn_nis /usr/tcb/bin/XSysAdmin
login
passwd


8 Files

8.1 ASE start script

#
#  A skeleton example of a start action script.
#

PATH=/sbin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/nm2000/bin
export PATH
ASETMPDIR=/var/ase/tmp

if [ $# -gt 0 ]; then
        svcName=$1              # Service name to start
else
        svcName=
fi

#
# Reconfigure NIS to run master
#
/sbin/init.d/nis stop
NIS_DOMAIN=`/usr/sbin/rcmgr get NIS_DOMAIN`
/usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_TYPE MASTER
/usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_ARGS "-s -S $NIS_DOMAIN,localhost"
/usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_PASSWDD YES
/sbin/init.d/nis start

#
# Edit /etc/hosts
#
ON=NIS_MASTER
OFF=NIS_SLAVE
HOSTS=/etc/hosts
cp $HOSTS $HOSTS.old
sed  -e 's/^#\(.*\)\(# '$ON'\)/\1\2/' -e 's/\(^[^#].*# '$OFF'\)/#\1/'\
< $HOSTS.old > $HOSTS

#
#  Any non zero exit will be considered a failure.
#
exit 0

8.2 ASE stop script


#
#  A skeleton example of a stop action script.
#

PATH=/sbin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/nm2000/bin
export PATH
ASETMPDIR=/var/ase/tmp

if [ $# -gt 0 ]; then
        svcName=$1              # Service name to stop
else
        svcName=
fi

#
# This script runs when the service is stopped, and also when the ASE
# is initializing on a member as it boots.  You can test the context:
#
case "${MEMBER_STATE}" in
BOOTING | RUNNING)
        #
        # Reconfigure NIS to run slave.
        #
        NIS_DOMAIN=`/usr/sbin/rcmgr get NIS_DOMAIN`
        /sbin/init.d/nis stop
        /usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_TYPE SLAVE
        /usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_ARGS "-s -S $NIS_DOMAIN,localhost"
        /usr/sbin/rcmgr set NIS_PASSWDD NO
        /sbin/init.d/nis start

        #
        # Edit /etc/hosts
        #
        OFF=NIS_MASTER
        ON=NIS_SLAVE
        HOSTS=/etc/hosts
        cp $HOSTS $HOSTS.old
        sed  -e 's/^#\(.*\)\(# '$ON'\)/\1\2/' -e 's/\(^[^#].*# '$OFF'\)/#\1/'\
        < $HOSTS.old > $HOSTS
        ;;
esac

#
#  exit 0  = success - service stopped successfully
#  exit 1  = failure - could not stop service
#  exit 99 = failure - could not stop service (service busy)
#
exit 0

8.3 /var/yp/pollservers

Note that SERVICE=tmn_nis is hardcoded. It could be put into
rc.config.


#!/sbin/sh
PATH=$PATH:/bin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/usr/sbin:/var/yp

SERVERFILE=/var/yp/src/ypservers
TIMEOUT=10
MAPDIR=/tmp/ypservers$$
SERVICE=tmn_nis

[ -f $SERVERFILE ] || exit 0
mkdir $MAPDIR || exit 0

for Y in `cat $SERVERFILE`
do
        if ping -c 1 $Y >/dev/null 2>&1
        then
                > $MAPDIR/$Y
        fi >> $MAPFILE &
done

sleep $TIMEOUT

ls $MAPDIR | awk '{print $0, $0}' | makedbm \
-m $SERVICE - /var/yp/`domainname`/ypservers
yppush ypservers

rm -rf $MAPDIR

8.4 /var/yp/src/ypservers

rip.dscc.dk
rap.dscc.dk
rup.dscc.dk

8.5 root crontab

# Network Information Service: SLAVE server entries 
30 * * * * sh /var/yp/ypxfr_1perhour
31 1,13 * * * sh /var/yp/ypxfr_2perday
32 1 * * * sh /var/yp/ypxfr_1perday
# Network Information Service: MASTER server entries
* * * * * /var/yp/pollservers

8.6 Makefile

Note that SERVICE=tmn_nis is hardcoded. 

...
..
YPDBDIR=/var/yp
YPPUSH=$(YPDIR)/yppush
#MAKEDBM=$(YPDIR)/makedbm
MAKEDBM=$(YPDIR)/makedbm -m tmn_nis
REVNETGROUP=$(YPDIR)/revnetgroup
NONSEC=_nonsecure
..

