           DEVICE STORAGE SHELF

    In the above diagram=2C there is a storage shelf connected to SCSI Port=
 1
    on the HSZ40=2E Each physical slot in the storage shelf is assigned a
    SCSI =22ID=22 number=2C this would be the Device Side Target ID=2E  In =
a =22non split=22
    bus configuration=2C the target ID=27s in a device storage shelf are nu=
mbered
    0 =2D 6=2C from right to left; as viewed from the the front of the stor=
age
    shelf=2E In a single HSZ40 configuration=2C SCSI ID 7 or 6 is used by t=
he HSZ40=2E
    In a dual HSZ40 configuration=2C SCSI IDs 6 and are used by the HSZ40=
=2E

    In a dual controller configuration=2C no SCSI devices can be plugged in=
to
    storage shelf slots that have an ID of 6=2E In a single controller
    configuration=2C if a controller is plugged into the controller shelf s=
lot
    that uses SCSI ID 6=2C no SCSI devices can be plugged into storage shel=
f
    slots that have that same ID of 6=2E

    The first step in making a disk unit on the HSZ40 is to configure a dis=
k
    device=2E  This is done by giving the disk device a name and then assoc=
iating
    that name with SCSI information that will allow the HSZ40 to relate the
    name to a =22device bus=22 and a =22target=22 on that bus=2E  In the do=
cumentation
    you may see the disk =22name=22 defined as a =22container=2Dname=22=2E

    The configuring of a disk device is done by =22adding=22 a disk=2E Ther=
e are
    three ways of adding a disk device=3A

    1=2E Through the =22add disk=22 HSZ40 CLI command
    2=2E Through the =22config=22 utility on the HSZ40
    3=2E Through the =22cfmenu=22 utility on the HSZ40

    The config and cfmenu utilities invoke the add disk command=2E

    There is a fourth method=2C but it uses the HSZterm layered product
    utility=2C which will not be be used in this document=2E It requires th=
at
    a running OSF=2F1 system be connected through the terminal port on the =
HSZ40=2E

    The creation of disk devices uses the =22Port Target Lun =22=28PTL=29 n=
aming
    convention=2E  The =22Port=22 refers to the Device SCSI Port=2E The =22=
Target=22 refers
    to the =22target=22 ID number of the storage shelf associated with the =
physical
    slot that contains the device=2E The =22LUN=22 is always =220=22=2C in =
the case of the
    HSZ40=2E
=0C                                                                       P=
age 7

    The disk name can be up to nine characters=2C see the HSx users
    guide for restrictions=2E  The name is referred to as =22container=2Dna=
me=2E=22
    The standard convention for the name is to use the following format=3A

    =22DiskPTL=22=2C an example is Disk100=2E

    The =22P=22 refers to the SCSI Bus Port number=2E
    The =22T=22 refers to the SCSI ID number represented by the physical sl=
ot
    location in which the device is located=2E
    The =22L=22 is the LUN number=2E In the case of the HSZ40=2C the LUN is=
 always 0=2E

    Name                Port    Target  Lun

    Disk100              1        0      0=2E

    The format of the HSZ40 CLI command that would be used would be=3A

    hsz40> add disk =22name=22 Port Target Lun

    EXAMPLE=3A

    hsz40>add disk disk100 1 0 0

    However=2C you could assign any container name you wished=2E Example

    Name                Port    Target  Lun

    DEV1                 1        0      0=2E

    EXAMPLE 2=3A

    Consider another storage shelf connected to SCSI Port 6 and there
    was a disk in slot ID 5 of that shelf=2E You would add the disk using t=
he
    standard name format with the following HSZ40 command=2E

    hsz40>add disk disk650 6 5 0

    Name                Port    Target  Lun

    Disk650              6        5      0=2E

    The config and cfmenu utilities automatically scan all DEVICE SCSI Port=
s
    connected to the HSZ40 Ports and configures all disks located in any
    storage shelves connected to those ports=2E These utilities use the PTL=
 name
    format=2E

