III=2DA=2E1    TRANSPORTABLE vs=2E NOTRANSPORTABLE

    When a single device container is created it can be defined as transpor=
table
    or notransportable=2C the default is NOtransportable=2E  This option is
    specified in the =22ADD DISK=22 command=2E

    hsz40>Add Disk Disk100 1 0 0 Transportable or NOtransportable

    A NOtransportable disk uses some of the disk space for metadata that is
    used by the HSZ40=2E A device that is specified as NOtransportable will=
 have
    a few less blocks available for user data=2E Refer to the HSxx users gu=
ide for
    more information on transportable=2Fnotransportable=2E

=0C
                                                                        Pag=
e 10
III=2DB=3A MULTI DEVICE CONTAINERS

   If RAID or STRIPE or MIRROR containers are going to be created=2C then t=
he
   disk devices must first be configured=2E  After disk devices are added=
=2C RAID
   or STRIPE or MIRROR containers can be configured from single disk contai=
ners=2E

   To create a RAID container=2C the ADD RAID command will be used=2E  To c=
reate
   a STRIPE container=2C the ADD STRIPE command will be used=2E
   The format of the commands are=3A

   hsz40>add raid =22container=2Dname=22 =22list of disk devices=22
   hsz40>add stripe =22container=2Dname=22 =22list of disk devices=22

   The =22container=2Dname=22 has the same restrictions as the disk names=
=2E See the
   HSxx Users Guide=2E

   EXAMPLES=3A

   hsz40>add raid r0 disk100 disk200 disk300
   hsz40>add stripe s0 disk110 disk210 disk310

   To view the containers=3A

   hsz40>show raid
   hsz40>show stripe

   The above commands show all containers=2E  To view an individual contain=
er=2C
   use the following command=3A

   hsz40>show r0
   hsz40>show s0

   For MIRROR containers refer to the HSXX Array Controller Users Guide=2E

=0C
                                                                        Pag=
e 11

III=2DC=3A INITIALIZATION OF CONTAINERS

   After containers have been created they should be initialized=2E

   These containers could be=3A

   1=2E Single Device Containers=2E
   2=2E RAID Containers=2E
   3=2E STRIPE Containers=2E
   4=2E MIRROR Containers=2E

   Example=3A

   hsz40>initialize disk100   ; This is for a single device container
   hsz40>initialize r0        ; This is for a RAID Container
   hsz40>initialize s0        ; This is for a STRIPE container

III=2DD=3A CREATION OF UNITS

  The unit is the storage container that will be made available to
  the host operating system=2E  Each container that is going to made availa=
ble
  to the host operating system must be given a unit designator through the
  =22ADD UNIT=22 Command=2E The format of the command is=3A

  hsz40>add unit =22unit designator=22 =22container=2Dname=22

  The container=2Dname must be a valid container=2E  The unit designator fo=
r a disk

  unit must have the following format=3A

  =22DTZL=22

  The first character must be a =22D=22 for disk=2E

  The =22TZL=22 can be from one to 3 numeric characters=2C with the followi=
ng
  restrictions=3A

  The =22T=22 character must be one of the Host Side SCSI ID=27s of the
  controller=2E That is=2C if the controller has Host Side SCSI ID=27s of 0=
=2C1=2C2=2C3=2C
  then the =22T=22 character can only take on a value of 0=2C1=2C2=2C or 3=
=2E

  The =22Z=22 character must be =220=22 or not used=2E Not used implies =22=
0=22

  The =22L=22 character can be a number between 0=2D7 or not used=2E Not us=
ed implies 0=2E

  Using this unit designator naming scheme=2C a controller can present a ma=
ximum
  of 32 units to the OSF=2F1 Host operating system=2E  That=27s four Host S=
ide
  SCSI IDs and 8 units per ID=2E The units refer to containers=2E  The cont=
ainers
  can be single device or multi=2Ddevice containers=2C such as RAID or STRI=
PE
  containers=2E

  EXAMPLE>=3A Controller has Host Side SCSI ID=27s of 0=2C1=2C2=2C3

  hsz40>add unit d0    disk100   ; for single device container
  hsz40>add unit d000  disk100   =3A
  hsz40>add unit d100  r0        ; for RAID container
  hsz40>add unit d200  s0        ; for STRIPE container
  hsz40>add unit d307  disk650

  The HSZ40 unit designator will be used to determine the OSF=2F1 minor num=
ber
  of the OSF=2F1 device special file that is used by OSF=2F1 to access that=
 HSZ40
  unit=2E  This minor number will be used in the mknod utility to associate
  an OSF=2F1 device name with an HSZ40 unit=2E

=0C                                                                       P=
age 12
IV=3A     OSF=2F1 SPECIAL FILES

   Once units have been made available to the OSF=2F1 Host System=2C then t=
he
   Host System can access those devices if the special files have been crea=
ted
   for the unit=2E

   Each individual partition of a OSF=2F1 disk device must have a character
   device special file and a block mode device special file=2E

   The device special files are created through the mknod utility on the
   OSF=2F1 Host System=2E  This section will show how that utility is used=
=2E

   The mknod utility must be supplied some parameters=2C those parameters
   include a major number and a minor number=2E  The major number refers to
   the device driver=2C the minor number refers to the specific device that
   is controlled by the device driver=2E  The format for invoking the mknod
   utility is=3A

    mknod name M  Major Number  Minor Number

    The =22name=22 is the name given to the special file associated with th=
e
    device and the partition on the device=2E

    The =22M=22 will be the character =22b=22 for block mode file or =22c=
=22
    for character mode file=2E

    The Major Number will be the character 8=2C for HSZ40 devices=2E

    The Minor Number will be a number that is determined from an algorithm
    that is based upon the SCSI bus number that is connected to the HSZ40
    and the HSZ40 unit designator=2E  The next sections will show examples=
=2E
=0C
                                                                        Pag=
e 13
IV=2DB =3A RECOMMENDED NAME FORMAT FOR HSZ40=2FOSF1 DEVICES

      The recommended naming format for HSZ40 devices is based upon the
      following format

      rzxnnp  =2D for block mode special file access
      rrzxnnp =2D for character mode special file access

      The =22x=22 denotes one alphabetic character that represents the LUN =
number
      that is used in each HSZ40 unit designator=2E

      LUN 0 =2D a
      LUN 1 =2D b
      LUN 2 =2D c
      LUN 3 =2D d
      LUN 4 =2D e
      LUN 5 =2D f
      LUN 6 =2D g
      LUN 7 =2D h


      The =22nn=22 denotes a numeric value based on the Host Side SCSI bus =
=23 and
      the target ID portion of the HSZ40 unit number=2E

      nn =3D =28 8 * Host Side SCSI Bus =23 =29 =2B =28 Target ID from HSZ4=
0 unit number=29

      The =22p=22 denotes one alphabetic character between a=2Dh to designa=
te the
      partition=2E

      EXAMPLE 1=3A

      To configure HSZ40 unit D000 or D0 on an HSZ40 that is
      on Host Side SCSI Bus =23 2=2C the name for the block mode device wou=
ld be=3A

      nn =3D =28 8 * 2 =29 =2B 0 =3D 16
      x  =3D LUN 0 =3D a

      Block Mode Names=3A  rza16a =2D partition a
                         rza16b =2D partition b=2C etc

      EXAMPLE 2=3A

      To configure HSZ40 unit D407 on an HSZ40 that was
      on Host Side SCSI Bus =23 5=2C the name for this block mode device wo=
uld be=3A

      nn =3D =28 8 * 5 =29 =2B 4 =3D 44
      x  =3D LUN 7 =3D h

      Block Mode Names=3A  rzh44a =2D partition a
                         rzh44b =2D partition b=2C etc
