I've had a major ADVFS crash on a key server, with ADVFS reporting 'no
mcells on next page' followed immediately by a kernel panic and dump.  I
can mount /usr (the file system affected), and do some file manipulation,
but if I touch the wrong one, down I go.  I' attempting to back up what's
critical to tape, prior to doing a restore, but I'd prefer a different
option, if available.  Anyone with any ideas, let me know.

From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Nov 07 20:21:22 1997
From: System PRIVILEGED Account <root () dragon ! tricity ! wsu ! edu>
Date: Fri, 07 Nov 1997 20:21:22 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: ADVFS crash...
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078619210333


I've had a major crash on a key server.  ADVFS is reporting 'no
free mcells on next page' followed by a kernel panic and back to prompt.
This is one of the disk volumes on the /usr file system.  I can boot
single user and mount /usr, even manipulate some files, but if I touch the
wrong one, down I go.  I'm trying to back up the mail spool to tape as I
write this, in the event I have to do a restore of the /usr partition from
an old tape backup.  So far, that, at least, is working.  I've run msfsck
from /usr/field and turned up some 'bad bitmap' errors, vchkdir goes into
an apparently endless loop reporting 'possible lost file found'.  Any
suggestions other than a restore from tape would be very welcome.  Thanks.

